WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.900
Backpropagation.

2
00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:06.933
This is the single idea that makes deep learning computationally possible.

3
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:10.800
Training asks one question about every weight.

4
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.533
If I nudge this weight slightly, how much does the loss change?

5
00:00:14.833 --> 00:00:16.733
That number is the weight's gradient.

6
00:00:17.933 --> 00:00:19.633
There's a naive way to find them.

7
00:00:19.933 --> 00:00:22.168
Nudge each weight and rerun the network.

8
00:00:22.168 --> 00:00:25.800
With a million weights, that's a million forward passes per step.

9
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:31.800
Backprop gets every gradient in one backward sweep, for about twice the cost of a forward pass.

10
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:34.767
That efficiency is the whole reason this works.

11
00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:37.167
Here's the mechanism.

12
00:00:37.467 --> 00:00:40.367
On the forward pass, every layer caches its input.

13
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:44.000
Then, starting at the loss, an error signal flows backward.

14
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.500
At each layer, it yields that layer's weight gradients, then passes on to the layer below.

15
00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.300
Underneath is the chain rule.

16
00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.033
First, how the loss changes with the output.

17
00:00:55.333 --> 00:00:57.933
Then how the output changes with the hidden layer.

18
00:00:58.233 --> 00:01:01.726
And finally how the hidden layer changes with the weight.

19
00:01:01.726 --> 00:01:04.667
Multiply them, and never recompute a term twice.

20
00:01:05.867 --> 00:01:08.767
Here's a tiny network, differentiated by hand.

21
00:01:09.067 --> 00:01:12.033
The forward pass computes and remembers each value.

22
00:01:12.333 --> 00:01:16.100
Then we start at the loss and apply local derivatives, right to left.

23
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:18.567
That gives the gradient for the second weight.

24
00:01:18.867 --> 00:01:21.436
The signal keeps flowing down to the first.

25
00:01:21.436 --> 00:01:23.767
These match PyTorch's backward exactly.

26
00:01:24.967 --> 00:01:26.633
Here's backprop paying rent.

27
00:01:26.933 --> 00:01:29.867
Your custom focal loss turns to NaN at step forty.

28
00:01:30.167 --> 00:01:31.678
You know where to look.

29
00:01:31.678 --> 00:01:37.000
The log of p has a derivative of one over p, which explodes as p approaches zero.

30
00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:40.819
An infinite signal gets multiplied through every layer.

31
00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:44.467
One clamp fixes it, instead of re-architecting the model.

32
00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:46.398
Let's recap.

33
00:01:46.398 --> 00:01:49.567
A gradient says how much each weight moves the loss.

34
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:52.200
Backprop gets all of them in a single sweep.

35
00:01:52.500 --> 00:01:54.633
Forward caches, backward consumes.

36
00:01:54.933 --> 00:01:57.646
It's just the chain rule, well organized.

37
00:01:57.646 --> 00:01:59.367
Next up: gradient descent.
